  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/9/9a/Amazing_Spider-Man_Vol_1_8.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "As Midtown High's science teacher wheels in a strange-looking robot device, Flash shoves Peter, causing his glasses to fall off and shatter.&nbsp; Though Peter acknowledges that he no longer needs the glasses, he's frustrated at taking the constant abuse from Flash.&nbsp; The teacher then introduces Mr. Petty of the \"I.C.M Corporation,\" who shows off the \"Living Brain.\"&nbsp; Mr. Petty has Peter Parker step forward to ask it a question, and the class prompts him to ask what Spider-Man's real identity is.&nbsp; Peter is terrified that it might be smart enough to guess correctly!\n\nWhile they watch, the two robot attendants consider how owning something like the Living Brain could be used to predict horse races and so forth.&nbsp; They decide to steal it.&nbsp; Peter feeds the information into the Brain, and out comes a coded piece of paper that he's given the job to translate overnight.&nbsp; Flash tries to take it from him, but Peter holds him back, and the science teacher suggests they settle the matter \"in the gym!\"\n\nAfter class, with his boxing trunks on, Peter is worried he'll hurt Flash.&nbsp; But his spider abilities make him able to move so fast that Flash can't even get close, and it looks to the watching students that he's simply staying out of Flash's reach.&nbsp; Finally, Peter uses only the tiniest fraction of his power and throws a punch, and Flash is thrown out of the ring, completely dazed.&nbsp; Thinking it was just a lucky punch, he reengages.\n\nBut while they're fighting, the two attendants make their move to steal the machine.&nbsp; They push Petty away and accidentally bump the machine, which seems to come to life!&nbsp; It begins to run wild through the school.&nbsp; Meanwhile, in the gym, Flash is realizing that no matter how hard he tries to connect, Peter seems able to dodge every punch.&nbsp; Peter decides to just flick his wrist to KO Flash, but there is commotion from outside the gym just as he throws his punch, and Flash turns to look, so it appears to everyone that it's a foul.&nbsp; Peter struck while his opponent's head was turned!\n\nPeter carries Flash to the locker room, and soon Spider-Man is on the scene to confront the robot, which is running amok through the hallways.&nbsp; Spider-Man probes and tests and begins to realize that the Brain is learning and is very likely to be able to predict his movements before long.&nbsp; He continues to evade it while occasionally having to stop to let kids out of the school.&nbsp; The Brain is learning more and more and is becoming a threat even to Spider-Man.\n\nBut the hero eventually jumps for the robot's controls, managing to finally deactivate it.\n\nThe two attendants run for cover but bump into the now recovered Flash Thompson, who is getting dressed in the locker room.&nbsp; They are accidentally knocked out.&nbsp; Everyone sees what looks like Flash capturing the two crooks, and Peter can't resist suggesting that he's Spider-Man.&nbsp; For starters, he wasn't around when the robot attacked, he tried to take the secret identity paper from him, and of course, it had to look like he lost the fight to Peter Parker so that no one would suspect who he really is!&nbsp; Peter smirks as Flash continues to try and deny it.\n\nOn his way home from school, Peter decides to claim he lost the paper during all the excitement.&nbsp; And the day wasn't a complete failure, as he got to clobber Flash without giving himself away.&nbsp; \"It's been a mighty pleasant day!\"",
  "grade_one_liner": "Strong character work and a <b>great boxing sequence</b> keep the issue engaging.\nThe repetitive 9-panel robot slog caps it below upper-mid-grade.",
  "subtitle": "A Knockout in the Gym -- A Snoozer in the Hallway",
  "review_text": "[SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=bold; text=FOR THE RECORD:]\n[BR] This is a review of the <b>first</b> of the two stories found in this magazine.\n[BR] My review on the other story is posted elsewhere.\n\n[DIVIDER]\nA mostly solid effort that stays relevant more for the boxing match between Peter Parker and Flash Thompson (which, I suspect, is what the reading audience really <i>cared</i> about) than for the largely lackluster battle between Spider-Man and... <i>well</i>... a [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold; text=robot], living or otherwise.\n\n[ASSET: left | MyGlasses | width=15%] There is no <i>question</i> that the memorable moments of this issue all stem from the conflict between Flash and Peter, starting with the minor world-building moment where the reader learns that Peter no longer needs his glasses (presumably because of his enhanced spider abilities).\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=After all, why <i>else</i> would he make the claim \"I don't <i>need</i> those specs anyway!\"]\n[BR] It's a nice, quiet reminder of how the strip evolves Peter in small increments.\n\nBut the backdrop of <i>everything</i> is the rolled-in presence of Mr. Petty (from the <b>ICM</b> corporation, Marvel's version of <b>IBM</b>, no doubt, <i>heh</i>), and the 1960s version of AI is unveiled in front of the high school class, with Peter faced with the unsettling possibility that the Living Brain might actually be advanced enough to deduce the true identity of Spider-Man.\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=It's the one bit of that part of the plot that is at least <b><i>compelling</i></b>.]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | SettleItInTheGym | width=9%] [ASSET: right | FlashBrained | width=18%] But the action quickly transitions back to the Flash/Peter rivalry, this time to be decided in the gym with boxing gloves on.&nbsp; And this is where Ditko provides one of the [SPAN: severity=success; text=best panels of the year] when Peter discovers that even the \"smallest fraction\" of his spider powers is enough to nearly knock Flash senseless and [SOUNDEFFECT: text=WHAM!]\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=success; text=<b><i> Bravo!</i></b>  Ditko's absolutely <i>perfect</i> drawing knocks the moment out of the park every bit as much as Flash was sent flying out of the ring!]\n[BR]\n[BR] He could not have drawn this moment better, in my opinion.\n\n[DIVIDER]\nSadly, the reader's attention is ripped away from this... much as Ditko and Lee abruptly pivot the narrative itself...&nbsp; to the far more mundane villain-of-the-month motif, this time with the two thuggish attendants' attempt to steal the Living Brain, and to no one's surprise, it begins to run amok through the school.\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Everyone!&nbsp; Run for your lives!]\n\n[ASSET: left | MoreCaution | width=12%] [ASSET: right | FlashKOed | width=12%] Thankfully, we at least get to see the conclusion of the boxing match, and Steve once again <i>nails</i> the facial expressions, with Flash's look of caution on the left and his KOed look on the right.\n[BR]\n[BR] Even though Peter is called for a foul, all I can say is:\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=success; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=Sensational work by Steve Ditko!]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nAs much as readers would have liked to have seen more of this, we must remember that the name of the magazine <i>is</i> [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Amazing Spider-Man], and as the story had already advanced to page 8, it was time for the web-slinger's dramatic appearance.&nbsp; And note, for the record, that this is the second time in four issues that Spider-Man has shown up in Midtown High (see [LINK:1963:ASM_4|ASM #4|2026-10-26] for the first one).\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; text=Unfortunately, neither the script nor artwork was up to the hype,] and the suggestion that a 1960s-era robot could actually <i>anticipate</i> its opponent's moves and adapt to his strengths, ultimately using them against him -- [SPAN: severity=error; text=it all falls flat.]\n\nOne can well imagine that this might have felt <i>plausible</i> to the audience of the day, but modern readers are more likely to roll their eyes at such a notion, charming though it may have seemed at the time.&nbsp; (And <i>yes</i>, I admit, that does conceivably cross the bounds of [PAGE: scenario] a bit.)\n\n[ASSET: left | UnvariedLayout | width=19%] It doesn't help that Ditko's unimaginative battle sequences contrast <i>sharply</i> with his excellent close-ups, and the 9-panel layout is <i>rarely varied</i> over the entire second half of the story, including [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold; text=four pages in a row] (13-16)!\n[BR]\n[BR] This further cements the notion that Ditko's true strengths as illustrator lie elsewhere -- the frequent down-to-earth nature of the strip in general -- and it's perhaps no surprise that the <i>Amazing Spider-Man</i> magazine is noted for its vast supporting cast, which Ditko <i>excels</i> at depicting, each one bringing their own unique charm to the book.\n[BR]\n[BR] But he simply does not possess Kirby-like pizzazz when it comes to dynamic action, despite the fact that Spider-Man's odd motions <i>do</i> lend themselves more to his style.\n\nI feel compelled to point this out here to reinforce the fact that editor Stan Lee [SPAN: severity=success; text=knew exactly what he was doing] by commissioning Steve Ditko to draw the strip in the first place.\n\n[DIVIDER]\nAfter the Living Brain is disposed of, Peter can't resist getting one final dig at his nemesis by suggesting that he, in fact, is Spider-Man:\n[ASSET: center | FlashIsSpiderMan | width=67%]\n\n[SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=It's funny] and allows the book to end on a high note.\n\n[ASSET: left | FacialExpressions | width=13%] Despite Peter's best effort to put Flash on the defensive, it should be noted that page 12 of June's [LINK:1963:ASM_4|ASM #4|2026-10-26] <i>clearly</i> shows Flash in the classroom with the other students, cheering for Spider-Man when he entered to challenge the Sandman.\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(I suspect Peter already <i>knew</i> this, but still wanted to have a little fun at the expense of the school bully.)]\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nIt goes without <i>question</i> that this story is more notable for the <b>boxing match</b> than for Spider-Man's encounter with the <i>very</i> forgettable Living Brain.\n\nI'm sure it was an [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=italic; text=absolute thrill] for the readers of the day to finally see Peter and Flash go at it.&nbsp; That part, and Peter's joke on Flash on the final page were both <i>well</i> done, and Ditko's especially wonderful middle panel on page 6 is [SPAN: severity=success; text=not to be missed.]\n\nBut the Living Brain throws cold water on the whole affair and seemed to exist solely for Spider-Man to have something to fight.&nbsp; I give the creators credit enough to have used it wisely to set up the boxing match, because otherwise, the battle sequences weren't noteworthy at all.\n\nEven <i>if</i> the Living Brain could learn and predict (a tall order projecting AI onto a 1960s goofy‑looking robot), since when could Spider-Man not just grab hold of the hunk of metal and tear it to pieces?&nbsp; That was what did not make sense to me, and we get the standard handful of pages of mostly pointless [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=italic; text=wake-me-up-when-it's-over] action while the two spar.\n\nEven though the Living Brain part of the plot likely vaporizes in a reader's mind the moment the book is closed (and caps the issue's overall impact), the thrill of the boxing match lingers long afterward, and I, along with probably every reader of the day, find myself satisfied and eager to see what happens next!\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=Those scenes firmly anchor the resulting grade at a respectable 6.5.]",
  "decoder_ring": "Note that <i>Web of Spider-Man</i> #35 (Feb 1988 cover date) pays homage to this cover and to the story to some degree in an issue that was pretty decent its own right, but of course, we'll get there <i>much </i>later!",
  "highlight": "Peter's boxing match with Flash Thompson highlights a story that is otherwise detoured by a runaway robot in the school hallways."
}